WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.249 --> 00:00:17.972
In the library that morning, Mr.

2
00:00:18.032 --> 00:00:21.653
Rochester informed me that we were to be married in four weeks.

3
00:00:23.013 --> 00:00:27.094
"Why did you take such pains to make me believe that you wished to marry Miss Ingram?"

4
00:00:27.554 --> 00:00:28.095
I asked.

5
00:00:29.315 --> 00:00:32.736
"I wanted to make you fall as madly in love with me as I was with you.

6
00:00:33.976 --> 00:00:36.577
I knew jealousy was my best ally in that cause."

7
00:00:37.858 --> 00:00:42.540
I didn't think this was fair to Miss Ingram, but he explained again that she had been more

8
00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.261
in love with his money than with him.

9
00:00:45.401 --> 00:00:46.722
I asked him to tell Mrs.

10
00:00:46.782 --> 00:00:50.203
Fairfax that we were engaged when she saw us kissing last night.

11
00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:55.145
He did so immediately, but I still felt that she didn't approve.

12
00:00:55.165 --> 00:01:01.288
"Gentlemen don't usually marry their governesses," she sniffed.

13
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.581
That morning Mr.

14
00:01:03.641 --> 00:01:07.344
Rochester took me to Millcote to purchase some new clothes and jewelry.

15
00:01:09.166 --> 00:01:13.169
The more he bought me, the more my cheeks burned with annoyance and shame.

16
00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:20.155
Suddenly I remembered my uncle, John Eyre, and his intention to adopt me and make me his heir.

17
00:01:21.296 --> 00:01:23.457
I decided to write to him about my marriage.

18
00:01:24.578 --> 00:01:29.843
Perhaps one day he would leave me an inheritance, and then I would be able to approach Mr.

19
00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:31.304
Rochester as an equal.

20
00:01:32.464 --> 00:01:37.145
Four weeks passed very quickly, and it was now the night before my wedding.

21
00:01:38.246 --> 00:01:43.847
Mr. Rochester had been away from home on business since the previous day, and I was still waiting

22
00:01:43.907 --> 00:01:44.787
for him to return.

23
00:01:45.988 --> 00:01:49.889
I desperately needed to speak to him about something that had occurred the previous night.

24
00:01:50.989 --> 00:01:53.410
A strong wind had been blowing all day.

25
00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.971
I went outside and ran through the orchard with the wind at my back.

26
00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:03.146
When I came to the wreck of the chestnut tree, the moon appeared momentarily.

27
00:02:05.027 --> 00:02:08.610
Half-covered by clouds, its disk was the color of blood.

28
00:02:09.831 --> 00:02:13.534
I went back to the house to make sure that a fire had been lit in the library

29
00:02:13.894 --> 00:02:15.135
in anticipation of Mr.

30
00:02:15.195 --> 00:02:16.416
Rochester's arrival.

31
00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:21.180
Feeling more restless than ever, I ran down to the gates.

32
00:02:22.401 --> 00:02:24.964
Tears filled my eyes when it began to rain.

33
00:02:24.984 --> 00:02:27.225
"I wish he would come!"

34
00:02:27.666 --> 00:02:30.048
I exclaimed, seized with foreboding.

35
00:02:31.208 --> 00:02:35.851
I opened the gates, determined to walk until I met him, and soon enough I did.

36
00:02:36.971 --> 00:02:41.214
He pulled me up onto his horse, and together we rode back to Thornfield.

37
00:02:42.434 --> 00:02:45.456
After we had changed into dry clothes, I met Mr.

38
00:02:45.516 --> 00:02:46.997
Rochester in the library.

39
00:02:47.017 --> 00:02:53.000
"What a bright spot of color you have on each cheek," he said, "and how strangely your eyes

40
00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.021
glitter! Are you well?"

41
00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:59.584
"I believe I am, but I must tell you about yesterday.

42
00:03:00.796 --> 00:03:03.177
All day I was very busy and very happy.

43
00:03:04.337 --> 00:03:07.698
It's a glorious thing to think about living with you because I love you."

44
00:03:08.938 --> 00:03:12.719
Here he wanted to kiss me, but I needed to press on with my story.

45
00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:19.301
"At sunset Adele and her nurse, Sophie, called me upstairs to look at my wedding dress, which had

46
00:03:19.341 --> 00:03:26.743
just arrived Underneath the dress I also found your gift, the extravagantly embroidered veil, which you had sent

47
00:03:26.783 --> 00:03:27.583
for from London."

48
00:03:27.603 --> 00:03:30.708
"You look so mournful," said Mr.

49
00:03:30.748 --> 00:03:37.432
Rochester. "Did you find something in the veil besides its embroidery—poison or a dagger perhaps?"

50
00:03:37.452 --> 00:03:39.413
"No, sir.

51
00:03:40.614 --> 00:03:45.897
Beyond the delicacy and richness of the fabric, I found nothing except your pride, and that did not

52
00:03:45.997 --> 00:03:47.498
scare me because I'm used to that."

53
00:03:49.359 --> 00:03:50.820
I continued with my story.

54
00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:56.303
For some time after I'd gone to bed, I could not sleep, and when I did sleep, I

55
00:03:56.343 --> 00:03:58.084
had a series of bad dreams.

56
00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.521
One was so upsetting that I woke up.

57
00:04:02.701 --> 00:04:05.062
I saw a candle burning on my dressing table.

58
00:04:06.302 --> 00:04:09.663
The door to my closet was open, and I heard a rustling within.

59
00:04:10.923 --> 00:04:15.265
Before going to bed, I had hung my wedding dress and veil there, so I thought it was

60
00:04:15.345 --> 00:04:17.605
morning and Sophie had come to help me dress.

61
00:04:18.826 --> 00:04:20.006
I called Sophie's name.

62
00:04:21.286 --> 00:04:26.068
No one answered, but a figure emerged from the closet and held the light aloft.

63
00:04:27.168 --> 00:04:28.549
"My blood ran cold, Mr.

64
00:04:28.589 --> 00:04:31.752
Rochester, because this was not Sophie or Mrs.

65
00:04:31.812 --> 00:04:35.615
Fairfax or Leah or even that strange woman, Grace Poole."

66
00:04:35.635 --> 00:04:39.258
"It must have been one of them," he insisted.

67
00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:45.243
"I solemnly assure you that I have never seen this creature before at Thornfield."

68
00:04:45.263 --> 00:04:48.066
"Describe her, Jane."

69
00:04:49.407 --> 00:04:55.292
"She was a tall, large woman with long, dark hair and dressed all in white."

70
00:04:56.448 --> 00:04:57.329
"Did you see her face?"

71
00:04:57.349 --> 00:05:01.952
"Not at first, but then she took my veil from the closet.

72
00:05:03.113 --> 00:05:07.776
She held it up, gazed at it for a long time, threw it over her head, and turned

73
00:05:07.796 --> 00:05:08.396
to the mirror.

74
00:05:09.597 --> 00:05:12.379
At that moment I saw her reflection quite clearly."

75
00:05:12.399 --> 00:05:14.941
"And what did she look like?"

76
00:05:14.961 --> 00:05:17.883
"I never saw such a ghastly face!

77
00:05:19.124 --> 00:05:25.388
It was discolored—purple with dark and swollen lips, a furrowed brow, and bloodshot eyes."

78
00:05:26.588 --> 00:05:28.129
"Ghosts are usually pale, Jane.

79
00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:30.430
What did this creature do?"

80
00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:37.613
"Sir, she removed my veil from her gaunt head, ripped the veil in two, and then trampled the

81
00:05:37.653 --> 00:05:38.533
pieces on the floor."

82
00:05:38.553 --> 00:05:40.034
"And then?"

83
00:05:40.054 --> 00:05:44.316
"She picked up the candle and retreated to the door.

84
00:05:45.516 --> 00:05:49.598
Just at my bedside, she thrust the candle up close to my face and blew it out.

85
00:05:50.718 --> 00:05:54.620
And for only the second time in my life, I fainted from terror.

86
00:05:55.804 --> 00:05:58.146
Now sir, tell me: who was that woman?"

87
00:05:58.166 --> 00:06:00.367
"I don't know, Jane.

88
00:06:01.528 --> 00:06:02.548
It must have been a dream."

89
00:06:02.568 --> 00:06:07.731
"That's what I thought, but when I rose this morning, I found my veil on the floor, torn

90
00:06:07.771 --> 00:06:09.272
in two from top to bottom."

91
00:06:10.473 --> 00:06:13.935
Mr. Rochester shuddered and hastily threw his arms around me.

92
00:06:13.955 --> 00:06:19.658
"Thank goodness that if anything harmful did come near you last night, it only damaged the veil.

93
00:06:20.939 --> 00:06:22.260
Oh, to think what might have happened!"

94
00:06:23.432 --> 00:06:26.174
He held me so tightly that I could hardly breathe.

95
00:06:26.194 --> 00:06:30.196
"Now I will explain everything to you," he said.

96
00:06:30.216 --> 00:06:38.361
"A woman did enter your room last night, and it was Grace Poole, but waking from your nightmare,

97
00:06:38.401 --> 00:06:40.402
you ascribed to her a goblin appearance.

98
00:06:41.723 --> 00:06:43.784
I know you wonder why I keep her in my house.

99
00:06:44.985 --> 00:06:49.288
When we have been married a year and a day, I will tell you—but not now."

100
00:06:50.504 --> 00:06:54.606
His answer seemed to be the only possible one, and it satisfied me.

101
00:06:55.827 --> 00:07:00.149
He didn't want me to sleep alone that night, so he made me promise to sleep with Adele

102
00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:02.791
in the nursery and to lock the door securely.

103
00:07:03.991 --> 00:07:05.752
That night I didn't sleep at all.

104
00:07:06.933 --> 00:07:10.055
With little Adele in my arms, I waited for the coming day.

105
00:07:11.335 --> 00:07:14.677
She seemed the emblem of my past life while Mr.

106
00:07:14.737 --> 00:07:19.520
Rochester was the dreaded but adored symbol of my unknown future.
